\title{The conditions mzLibR signals, and how to handle each}
\description{
Every failure mzLibR raises is an R condition whose class vector names \strong{what kind} of
failure it is, then \code{mzlib_error}, then \code{error}. Handle one kind by naming its class in
\code{\link{tryCatch}}; handle all of them by naming \code{mzlib_error}; or treat them as any other R error.
Nothing needs to be imported: \code{tryCatch()} dispatches on the class vector itself.
}
\details{
The classification is made in the \strong{bridge}, not in this package, so pyMzLib, mzLibRust and
mzLibR agree on it. Each verb's help page lists, under \strong{Errors}, which of these it raises and
when - generated from the bridge's verb spec, where the three kinds are called \code{usage},
\code{service_unavailable} and \code{correctness}.
}
\section{The classes}{


\code{mzlib_usage_error} - the call was malformed: a missing or invalid argument, a file that is not
there, a file with no such view. Raised before any work happens, either by mzLibR itself or by
the bridge (its \code{usage} kind, exit status 2). Fix the call; retrying will not help. pyMzLib
raises \code{UsageError}, mzLibRust returns \code{MzLibError::Usage}.

\code{mzlib_service_unavailable} - an external service is down, rate-limited, timing out or
unreachable: HTTP 408, 429 and 5xx from PRIDE, UniProt or EBI. \strong{Retry later.} A 404 or 400 is
deliberately not this class, because a wrong URL or a malformed request is a defect, and
excusing it as an outage would hide it. The live tests skip on this class and nothing else.
pyMzLib raises \code{ServiceUnavailableError}.

\code{mzlib_bridge_error} - mzLib itself reported a failure (the spec's \code{correctness} kind): a file
it could not parse, a vendor library that will not load on this platform. Its \code{error_type}
field carries the .NET exception type, e.g. \code{"HttpRequestException"}, so a handler can tell
failures apart without parsing prose. pyMzLib raises \code{BridgeError}.

\code{mzlib_project_not_found} - PRIDE has no project with that accession. PRIDE answers an unknown
accession with an empty list rather than a 404, and an empty list is indistinguishable from a
project with nothing matching, so this is raised rather than returning \code{list()}.

\code{mzlib_timeout} - the bridge did not finish within \code{timeout} seconds. Deliberately \strong{not}
\code{mzlib_service_unavailable}: a timeout can mean a slow service, but equally a wedged bridge, a
corrupt executable or a timeout set too short, and mzLibR will not guess which. Its \code{seconds}
field is the limit that was exceeded.

\code{mzlib_bridge_not_found} - no bridge executable could be located, or it could not be run. The
message names the three ways to provide one; see \code{\link{mzlibr_install_bridge}} and
\code{\link{mzlibr_bridge_path}}.

\code{mzlib_protocol_error} - the bridge answered with something this version cannot read: no output
from a process that died (stderr is quoted in the message), text that is not JSON, or a wire
protocol other than the one mzLibR speaks. Usually a bridge and a package from different
releases; \code{\link{mzlibr_bridge_version}} says which.

}
\section{Fields}{


Every condition has \code{message} and a \code{NULL} \code{call} - the useful context is in the message, not
in a call stack pointing at the transport. \code{mzlib_bridge_error} and \code{mzlib_service_unavailable}
add \code{error_type}; \code{mzlib_timeout} adds \code{seconds}.

}
\seealso{
\code{\link{mzlibr_bridge_version}}, \code{\link{mzlibr_install_bridge}}
}
\examples{

# A malformed call is refused before anything runs.
tryCatch(
  readers_read_spectra("run.mzML", limit = -1),
  mzlib_usage_error = function(e) conditionMessage(e)
)

# Every class also carries mzlib_error, so one handler can catch them all.
old <- options(mzlibr.bridge = file.path(tempdir(), "no-such-bridge"))
failure <- tryCatch(mzlibr_bridge_version(), mzlib_error = function(e) e)
class(failure)
options(old)
}
